## H3 — Stack buffer claim vs list/slice
| Artifact | What it says / does |
|----------|---------------------|
| `SPRINT.oot` 7.1 | “**stack buffer** (no per-char OoStr leak)” |
| Source comment | “`str_slice` once per token” |
| `.oo` implementation | scalars + `str_slice` — **no** `char buf[N]`, no `List` inside the walker |
| Lowered C | `OoStr name = oo_str_slice(args, start, i)` — **heap copy of the span** |
| `c_emit_call_args` | returns a **5-slot List** `[pos, args, a0, a1, a2]` — packing only |
| `t1_memory.oot` | already: “7.1 style = **slice, not stack C buffer**; language has no char stack array in `.oo`” |

Honesty: the **CRIT** (no per-char leak) is met. The **mechanism name** “stack buffer” was never landed and cannot be landed in `.oo` without a byte cursor / C helper. Path-A floor = **slice**, not a C VLA.

**H3: wording residual only. Do not FAIL the memory floor for missing stack `char[]`.**

